    "narrative": "Our offensive core is anchored today by **Kyle Tucker** (73.86) and **Josh Jung** (68.56), both of whom benefit from favorable matchups against pitchers with xERAs north of 4.50. **Kyle Tucker** remains our most reliable asset, with the model accounting for a solid SP skill boost against Canning. Meanwhile, **Josh Jung** is a standout play because of his 19% boost derived from facing the struggling Peralta; the park factors provide a significant lift to his ceiling, making him an elite start in this 5x5 format. At the top of the order, **Sam Antonacci** (61.94) earns a start over many of our middle-tier hitters; his 0.400 xwOBA and the premium associated with his lead-off spot make him a high-efficiency play today, even against Miller.\n\nThe most difficult decision on today\u2019s board was the final utility spot, where **Nolan Schanuel** (54.92) was narrowly edged out by **Andy Pages** (60.03) and **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** (60.88). While **Nolan Schanuel** offers a stable floor, his lower position in the batting order penalized his aggregate efficiency score, pushing him below the threshold required to crack a starting lineup that prioritizes run-scoring opportunities. Conversely, we are carrying **Nasim Nunez** (40.57) at shortstop\u2014a move necessitated by his elite stolen base potential. While his score is dangerously close to our \"Below Floor\" limit, his ability to swipe bags keeps him in the lineup despite the negative impact of his #9 spot in the order and the efficiency tax of facing a sub-3.00 xERA arm in McLean.\n\nWe remain cautious regarding the matchup for **Gunnar Henderson** (58.15), who must contend with Jax\u2019s high-end SP skill. However, **Gunnar Henderson** retains significant value due to the park factors and the 85% stolen base probability window, which justifies his aggressive projection. We are continuing to monitor the status of **Jacob Wilson** and **Jordan Westburg**, both of whom remain sidelined on the IL. With *